Changes to sing-box v1.14.4 (GNU GPL v3.0 or later), made by the unbraid authors, last on 2026-11-18:
a stand-in (fake) address is never handed to a second name while the range is unchanged: once the range is used
up, lookups of new names are answered with a server failure instead, an address that still names a domain is
skipped even when the saved position is older (a start moves past such addresses), and libbox tells how many
addresses can still be handed out (FakeIPRemaining), so the app can move to a fresh range in time.

What is promised, and against what: within one table (one range in one cache file's life) an address is never
given to a second name. This holds across crashes (each name is committed before its address is handed out, and the
database commits atomically) and across every error the database reports (a failed read or write, a failed
transaction, a panic): the table then hands out nothing more, FakeIPRemaining is 0, no checkpoint survives, and a
cache file holding fake-IP names is never replaced by an empty one (found damaged at open, the start fails). The app
then moves to a new range with a deleted file. A cache file changed or corrupted without any reported error is out
of scope: it is the app's own private file, and the app deletes it at every connect, app start and range move.
The root zone (".") is answered with no address, as it has no name to save.
Applied by tools/build-libbox.sh; git apply skips these lines.

